class:: BlipInt
summary:: Finite inverse-harmonic phase integral of a Blip
categories:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
related:: Guides/BlipExtensions, Classes/BlipAD, Classes/BlipDecay, Classes/BlipDiff, Classes/BlipDisp, Classes/BlipFB, Classes/BlipFrac, Classes/BlipKernel, Classes/BlipPM, Classes/BlipPeak, Classes/BlipPower, Classes/BlipSeries, Classes/BlipWidth

description::

BlipInt sums code::sin(k * phase) / k:: and divides by the harmonic number
code::H_N::. The result is zero mean and broad or ramp-like. The harmonic
number is a conservative magnitude bound rather than an empirical gain.

The initial implementation is intentionally O(N). It generates harmonic phase
with one double-precision recurrence and uses a plugin-load-time reciprocal
table; there is no oscillator state per partial and no free-running
integrator, so zero and negative frequency do not drift. Topology changes
fade for one block, and the requested count stops at 2048. The retained
harmonics are limited below Nyquist and the linear inverse weighting produces
no additional sidebands.

The closest vanilla comparison is a bandlimited link::Classes/Saw::. Both
carry inverse-harmonic magnitudes, but BlipInt uses sine phase, finite explicit
count, and harmonic-number bound normalization.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: freq
Frequency in Hz. Audio or control rate; signed values are valid.

argument:: numharm
Requested harmonic count, sampled once per block and clipped to code::1..2048::
before Nyquist limiting.

argument:: mul
Post-processing multiplier.

argument:: add
Post-processing offset.

examples::

code::
(
{ BlipInt.ar(100, 40, 0.1) }.play;
)
::

code::
(
{ RLPF.ar(BlipInt.ar(60, 80), 1200, 0.2) * 0.1 }.play;
)
::

code::
// Modulating all parameters for a dynamic sound
(
{ BlipInt.ar(MouseX.kr(50, 800, 'exponential'), LFNoise1.kr(1).range(1, 100)) ! 2 * 0.1 }.play;
)
::
